<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Elisabethenburg Palace</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1682">1682</a>–<a href="/years/1692">1692</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Schloss Elisabethenburg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Elisabethenburg Palace is the former residence of the dukes of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiningen">Meiningen</a> in Meiningen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>. Duke Bernhard I built it between 1682 and 1692 and named it after his second wife, Elisabeth Eleonore. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> wings remained the seat of the ducal court until 1918 and now house the Meiningen museums, with collections on the court <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and music.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The palace stands on the north-western edge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiningen">Meiningen</a>, on the site of a late-Gothic castle built in 1511. Part of that castle, the Bibrasbau, named after Bishop Lorenz von Bibra, was built into the north wing of the new building. Bernhard I, the first Duke of Saxe-Meiningen, gave it three wings and a central tower, which together form an E shape when seen from above. The work was carried out between 1682 and 1692.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The building was remodelled in the 19th century, when a dry moat was added. The heavy rusticated window surrounds that now dominate the facade date from 1845; the windows first had plain rectangular sandstone frames. An Italian marble fountain followed in 1918. Inside, the decoration runs from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> frescoes commissioned by Bernhard I through Rococo to the Empire style of the 19th century. The palace church of the Holy Trinity holds the tombs of Bernhard I, Elisabeth Eleonore of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a> and several later dukes, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anthony-ulrich-duke-of-brunswick-wolfenbuttel">Anton Ulrich</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The palace and its court orchestra are linked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hans-von-bulow">Hans von Bülow</a>, Max Reger and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-brahms">Johannes Brahms</a>, who was a frequent guest. Its music archives hold autograph scores, many of them by Bach, and historical instruments, and its large court library was used by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-schiller">Friedrich Schiller</a> while he stayed in the area. Besides the museum, the building now houses the Max Reger archives and music school, the Thuringian State Archives, the Johannes Brahms concert hall and the ceremonial rooms of the town council.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Elisabethenburg Palace” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bernhard I, third son of Ernest the Pious, received Meiningen in the 1680 partition of the Saxe-Gotha inheritance and made the small town his residence, building <b>Elisabethenburg Palace</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-meiningen">Duchy of Saxe-Meiningen</a></li></ul></section>
